1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,695
Support opens a ticket: “checkout failed,
but we have no audit row saying it even
started.” Engineering replies: “of course
— the whole transaction rolled back.”
Operations replies:

2
00:00:12,815 --> 00:00:17,519
“then how do we prove the attempt?” Same
database.

3
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:30,504
Two different truths people need from
nested writes. Propagation is the dial
between those truths: when a transactional
method calls another, does the inner work
join the outer unit, or run in a
transaction of its own?

4
00:00:30,684 --> 00:00:48,852
Spring’s default is Propagation.REQUIRED.
No transaction on the thread? Start one.
Already inside one? Join it. Nested
REQUIRED collaborators share one fate. One
rollback undoes everyone who joined.

5
00:00:48,972 --> 00:01:00,539
Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW does the opposite
split. Suspend the outer transaction if
present, open a fresh one, commit or roll
it back on its own, then resume the outer.

6
00:01:00,659 --> 00:01:16,451
The inner commit can survive outer failure
— which is exactly what checkout audit
usually wants. Look at this java for a
moment. Look at this java for a moment.
Narrate the nested calls. Client enters
checkout.

7
00:01:16,572 --> 00:01:36,444
Spring opens T1.
auditService.record("CHECKOUT_STARTED", …)
goes through the audit bean’s proxy.
REQUIRES_NEW suspends T1, opens T2,
inserts the audit, commits T2, resumes T1.
Purchase saves on T1. Payment throws.

8
00:01:36,564 --> 00:01:49,908
T1 rolls back — the purchase is gone. The
started audit remains, because T2 already
committed. Operations gets the breadcrumb.
Finance does not get a phantom purchase.

9
00:01:50,028 --> 00:02:09,756
Flip only the propagation on record to
plain @Transactional — still REQUIRED. The
audit insert joins T1. Payment fails.
Rollback erases “CHECKOUT_STARTED” too.
Support sees silence. Same methods.
Different nesting rule.

10
00:02:09,876 --> 00:02:20,099
Different database truth. Other modes
exist for sharper contracts. SUPPORTS
joins if present, otherwise runs non-
transactional.

11
00:02:20,219 --> 00:02:33,707
NOT_SUPPORTED suspends and runs without a
transaction — long reads that should not
hold locks. MANDATORY demands an existing
transaction and fails otherwise. NEVER
refuses to run inside one.

12
00:02:33,828 --> 00:02:46,403
NESTED uses a savepoint inside the same
physical transaction when the manager
supports it, so a partial rollback can
return to that mark without undoing the
entire outer unit. Keep the interview
sentence short.

13
00:02:46,524 --> 00:03:01,163
Propagation is not “how many databases.”
It is “what happens when transactional
methods nest on one thread.” The proxy and
TransactionInterceptor read the attribute
before the PlatformTransactionManager
begins, joins,

14
00:03:01,283 --> 00:03:04,019
suspends, or resumes.

15
00:03:04,139 --> 00:03:14,795
Misread it and rollbacks surprise you.
Catch an exception from a joined
participant, ignore that the outer
transaction is rollback-only, and wonder
why commit fails later.

16
00:03:14,915 --> 00:03:22,211
Call a REQUIRES_NEW method on this inside
the same class — no proxy, no suspension,
no independent commit.

17
00:03:22,331 --> 00:03:31,739
Design reviews usually debate only two
modes in practice: REQUIRED for shared
atomicity, REQUIRES_NEW when a side effect
must outlive outer failure.

18
00:03:31,859 --> 00:03:42,299
Joining or splitting still says nothing
about what concurrent transactions may
observe of each other’s rows while work is
in flight. That visibility question is
isolation.
